Below is a single‑file HTML page that does exactly what you asked for:  
* A button that the user can click.  
* When the button is pressed, a random joke is chosen from a list of four and shown on the page.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="UTF-8">
<title>Random Joke Generator</title>
<style>
  body {font-family: Arial, sans-serif; text-align: center; margin-top: 5rem;}
  #joke {margin-top: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.2rem; color:#333;}
  button {padding: 0.6rem 1.2rem; font-size: 1rem; cursor: pointer;}
</style>
</head>
<body>

<h1>Click the button for a joke!</h1>
<button id="jokeBtn">Tell me a joke</button>
<p id="joke"></p>

<script>
  // Array of four jokes
  const jokes = [
    "Why don’t scientists trust atoms? Because they make up everything!",
    "I told my wife she was drawing her eyebrows too high. She looked surprised.",
    "Why did the scarecrow win an award? Because he was outstanding in his field!",
    "I’m reading a book on anti‑gravity. It’s impossible to put down!"
  ];

  // Grab the button and the paragraph that will display the joke
  const btn = document.getElementById('jokeBtn');
  const jokeEl = document.getElementById('joke');

  // When the button is clicked, pick a random joke and display it
  btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
    const randomIndex = Math.floor(Math.random() * jokes.length);
    jokeEl.textContent = jokes[randomIndex];
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How to use
1. Copy the code above into a file named, for example, `joke.html`.  
2. Open that file in any web browser.  
3. Click the “Tell me a joke” button and enjoy a random joke each time!

**Solution Explanation**

The task is to print all elements that appear in *both* arrays.  
The only restriction is that we may **not** allocate any additional data
structures (no hash‑set, no extra vector, no map, …).

The classic way to solve this with only the two input arrays is:

1. **Sort** both arrays.  
   Sorting is allowed because it only rearranges the elements that are
   already stored in the input arrays – no new storage is needed.

2. Use two indices (`i` for the first array, `j` for the second) and walk
   through the sorted arrays simultaneously – the “two‑pointer” technique.

   * If `a[i] == b[j]` we found a common element → print it and advance
     both pointers.
   * If `a[i] < b[j]` the current element of the first array is too small,
     so we advance `i`.
   * Otherwise `b[j] < a[i]` and we advance `j`.

Because the arrays are sorted, this algorithm visits each element at most
once, giving a time complexity of  
`O(n log n + m log m)` (dominated by the two sorts) and only `O(1)` extra
memory.

The algorithm also handles duplicates correctly: if an element appears
`k` times in both arrays, the two‑pointer walk will encounter it `k` times
and print it `k` times.

---

#### Pseudocode

```
read n
read array A[0 … n-1]
read m
read array B[0 … m-1]

sort(A)
sort(B)

i = 0, j = 0
while i < n and j < m
    if A[i] == B[j]
        output A[i]
        i = i + 1
        j = j + 1
    else if A[i] < B[j]
        i = i + 1
    else
        j = j + 1
```

---

#### C++ Implementation

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    if (!(cin >> n)) return 0;          // no input
    vector<int> a(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) cin >> a[i];

    cin >> m;
    vector<int> b(m);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) cin >> b[i];

    sort(a.begin(), a.end());
    sort(b.begin(), b.end());

    int i = 0, j = 0;
    bool first = true;                 // for spacing
    while (i < n && j < m) {
        if (a[i] == b[j]) {
            if (!first) cout << ' ';
            cout << a[i];
            first = false;
            ++i; ++j;
        } else if (a[i] < b[j]) {
            ++i;
        } else {
            ++j;
        }
    }

    // If you prefer a newline at the end:
    cout << '\n';
    return 0;
}
```

**Input format**

```
n
a1 a2 ... an
m
b1 b2 ... bm
```

**Output**

All common elements separated by a single space (in ascending order).  
If there are no common elements, the program prints an empty line.

---

#### Why no extra data structures are used

* The only containers are the two input vectors `a` and `b`, which are
  required to hold the input data.
* The algorithm uses only a few integer variables (`i`, `j`, `first`).
* No additional arrays, hash tables, or other containers are created.

Thus the program satisfies the “no extra data structures” requirement.
